      "evidence": "**Evidence for each level:**\n- **High Risk:** AFGE reports the Census Bureau lost **~1,300\u20131,500 staff since January 2025** (against a September 2024 base of ~13,230 \u2014 roughly 10\u201315%), mostly via deferred resignation/early retirement; AP reports at least 15% staff loss. **Leadership removed:** Director Robert Santos resigned February 14, 2025; career acting director Ron Jarmin was replaced in September 2025 by political appointee George Cook (who simultaneously holds two other Commerce roles and, critics note, lacks the statutory qualifications); at least five division/office chiefs departed by April 2025. ~100 RIF notices issued to Tucson call-center staff in October 2025. DOGE \"terminated\" five Census-conducted surveys ($16.5M) in May 2025 and is reviewing others \"one-by-one.\"\n- **Moderate Risk (candidates):** FY2026 funding was ultimately *favorable*: the enacted CJS bill (P.L. 119-74) provides $1.49B (+7.8% vs. FY2025), though the **Current Surveys and Programs account \u2014 which funds the CPS/HVS \u2014 was cut ~3.2%** ($329.0M \u2192 $318.5M), and the administration had requested a much deeper $40M cut to Current Demographic Statistics. A proposed (not enacted) appropriations rider limiting survey follow-up contacts would have directly undermined CPS/HVS field operations.\n- **No Known Issue:** Clearly inapplicable given staff losses exceeding 1,000 and leadership turnover.\n\n**Assessment: High Risk.** Two independent High triggers are met: 1,000+ staff lost and leadership removed/replaced. Although topline FY2026 funding rose, the account that pays for this survey shrank, and field-staffing shortages predate and outlast the budget cycle.\n\n---"
