      "evidence": "**Evidence by level:**\n- *Gone:* No \u2014 collection and publication continue; the most recent monthly release was September 18, 2026, with the next scheduled October 16, 2026, and a full pre-announced schedule through December 2027.\n- *High Risk:* No statutory publication deadline exists to miss (the G.17 is not statutorily mandated), and the key upstream ICR is current: QPC (OMB Control No. **0607-0175**, Form MQ-C2) was re-approved July 31, 2024, **expiration 07/31/2027**, status Active (reginfo.gov, ICR 202403-0607-006).\n- *Moderate Risk:* **Publication delays did occur.** Due to the fall 2025 federal government shutdown delaying source data from BLS and Census, the G.17 releases scheduled for **October 17, 2025 and November 18, 2025 were not published as scheduled**; they were ultimately issued December 3 and December 23, 2025 (with the December release combined), per the Fed's own notices (federalreserve.gov/feeds/g17.html and the December 3, 2025 release text). This squarely matches the rubric's \"typical or intended publication date missed and/or collection or publication delayed.\" Publication has since returned to schedule.\n- *No Known Issue:* Would apply on the strength of the active ICR and on-schedule 2026 releases \u2014 but the rubric is behavioral, and the missed dates are recent, documented fact.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** Two intended publication dates were missed within the past year. Mitigating factors: the cause was upstream (shutdown), not internal to the Fed; the Fed communicated transparently and caught up within weeks; no cycle was skipped entirely. The episode does, however, demonstrate the release's structural dependence on other agencies' data flows \u2014 a recurrence pathway that remains open."
