      "evidence": "**Evidence for each level:**\n\n- **High Risk:** Not applicable. Authorization is not vague \u2014 peanuts are one of the few commodities with a commodity-specific statistical statute (7 U.S.C. \u00a7951), alongside the Agricultural Marketing Act of 1946 (7 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a71621\u20131627) and 7 U.S.C. \u00a72204.\n- **Moderate Risk:** Could apply if one read the collection as merely \"not explicitly required by statute.\" But the data has a hard programmatic dependency with **no substitutes**: FSA/CCC's Weekly National Posted Prices for peanuts \u2014 which set marketing assistance loan repayment rates and loan deficiency payments under the statutory peanut program (7 U.S.C. \u00a78757 and successors; national loan rate of $390/ton) \u2014 are derived directly from this NASS series. There is no peanut futures market and no alternative weekly national farmer-stock price collection.\n- **No Known Issue:** Explicit named statutory authorization exists (7 U.S.C. \u00a7951), and the data is required for implementation of a federal program (peanut marketing assistance loans), with no substitute collections. Notably, when NASS discontinued the Mink Survey and Agricultural Labor Survey in August 2025 (90 FR 42560), the justifications hinged on *\"no statutory requirement\"* and *\"availability of other data sources\"* \u2014 criteria that Peanut Prices does **not** meet, suggesting it sits in the protected tier of NASS's portfolio.\n\n**Assessment: No Known Issue.** This dataset combines explicit commodity-specific statutory authorization, an irreplaceable role in administering a statutory farm program (reinforced by the 2025 legislation raising peanut loan rates through 2031), and no substitute data source. It is among the most defensible collections NASS produces.\n\n---"
